Looking at the diff: the only change is in the JSON-schema float resolver (`floatJsonTag`), removing the `?` that made the fractional part optional in `YAML_FLOAT_EXPLICIT_PATTERN` — `[-+]?[0-9]+(?:\.[0-9]*)?` became `[-+]?[0-9]+(?:\.[0-9]*)`.

That explicit pattern is only consulted when a scalar carries an *explicit* `!!float` tag under `JSON_SCHEMA` (`constructScalar` → `resolve(source, true, tagName)`; implicit resolution uses the untouched `YAML_FLOAT_IMPLICIT_PATTERN`, and CORE/YAML11/DUMP schemas use different tags). Per the YAML 1.2 JSON-schema float regex, the fraction and exponent are both optional, so the baseline resolves explicit tags on integer/exponent-only forms:

- `!!float 5` → `5`, `!!float -2` → `-2`, `!!float 1e3` → `1000` (baseline)
- After the patch, none of these match the pattern → `NOT_RESOLVED` → `YAMLException: cannot resolve a node with !<tag:yaml.org,2002:float> explicit tag`

So this turns previously-working loads into hard errors — an observable regression (the new pattern is a strict subset, so nothing new resolves). The library's own suite passes because it apparently never explicitly tags an integer-form scalar as `!!float` under `JSON_SCHEMA`.

